WEBVTT

00:00:00.232 --> 00:00:03.069
Four points sit exactly on the line y
equals x.

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:04.982
Add one mismatched point.

00:00:04.982 --> 00:00:09.069
Least squares swings the slope from one
down to zero point two.

00:00:09.069 --> 00:00:11.851
The pull of a point grows with how wrong
it is,

00:00:11.852 --> 00:00:15.108
so a single gross error can outweigh all
the good ones.

00:00:15.107 --> 00:00:17.019
And in vision this is normal:

00:00:17.019 --> 00:00:19.957
a matcher pairs corners that merely look
alike.

00:00:19.922 --> 00:00:22.147
A robust loss caps that pull.

00:00:22.147 --> 00:00:26.388
The Huber loss is quadratic near zero and
linear beyond a threshold,

00:00:26.389 --> 00:00:28.323
so its influence stops growing.

00:00:28.322 --> 00:00:32.209
Fit one constant to one, two, three, four
and forty.

00:00:32.209 --> 00:00:33.709
The mean says ten.

00:00:33.709 --> 00:00:38.047
Huber says three, because forty now pulls
exactly as hard as four.

00:00:38.011 --> 00:00:40.711
RANSAC does not fit everything at once.

00:00:40.711 --> 00:00:43.311
It draws just enough points to fix the
model:

00:00:43.311 --> 00:00:44.911
two, for a line.

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:46.146
It fits them exactly,

00:00:46.147 --> 00:00:49.912
then counts how many other points fall
within a small threshold.

00:00:49.911 --> 00:00:51.349
Those are the votes.

00:00:51.349 --> 00:00:55.661
Then it tries again, and again, and keeps
the line with the most votes.

00:00:55.661 --> 00:00:59.311
At the end it refits by least squares on
the inliers alone.

00:00:59.303 --> 00:01:01.016
So how many tries are enough?

00:01:01.016 --> 00:01:05.903
Suppose forty percent of the points are
outliers and each sample takes two.

00:01:05.903 --> 00:01:10.591
Roughly how many samples give ninety-nine
percent confidence of one clean sample?

00:01:10.591 --> 00:01:12.403
Pause and estimate it.

00:01:13.854 --> 00:01:17.793
A sample is clean with probability zero
point six squared,

00:01:17.794 --> 00:01:19.830
which is zero point three six.

00:01:19.829 --> 00:01:23.067
Solve for N, and eleven tries are enough.

00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:27.392
A four-point homography at fifty percent
outliers needs seventy-two.

00:01:27.392 --> 00:01:31.167
An eight-point model needs one thousand
one hundred and seventy-seven.

00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:33.329
That's why we use minimal models.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:38.180
Vision models are often nonlinear, so
after RANSAC we refine them.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:43.680
Gauss-Newton linearises the residuals and
solves a small least-squares problem at
each step.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.580
Start at theta equals one.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:47.880
One step lands at two point four.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.305
The next lands near two, and then it is
almost exact.

00:01:51.314 --> 00:01:54.252
Far from the solution, a full step can
overshoot.

00:01:54.252 --> 00:01:57.089
Levenberg-Marquardt adds lambda to the
diagonal.

00:01:57.089 --> 00:02:01.244
With lambda equal to five, the first step
shrinks to zero point seven,

00:02:01.245 --> 00:02:05.340
and the error is zero point six six,
better than one point six three.

00:02:05.339 --> 00:02:07.964
If a step helps, accept it and shrink
lambda.

00:02:07.964 --> 00:02:10.339
If not, reject it and grow lambda.

00:02:10.339 --> 00:02:11.677
So the pipeline is:

00:02:11.677 --> 00:02:15.589
match features, run RANSAC for the
inliers, then refine.
